      "reason": "Persistent radicular pain after conservative management",
      "note": "Sixteen weeks from onset. Focused neurological examination performed today: right extensor hallucis longus four out of five, previously five out of five; right ankle dorsiflexion five minus out of five; sensation reduced to light touch over the lateral right calf and dorsum of the foot in an L5 distribution; ankle reflexes two plus and symmetric; no clonus; gait antalgic with a mild right foot slap. Conservative management has consisted of eight weeks of supervised physical therapy with documented home exercise adherence, together with naproxen from February to May and gabapentin from March, and has failed. Oswestry unchanged and there is now an objective motor deficit that was not present in February. Requesting magnetic resonance imaging of the lumbar spine without contrast. The result will determine management directly: a compressive lesion at L4-5 concordant with the examination goes to spine surgery referral, and an absence of compression goes to an image-guided injection rather than an operation.",
